   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">That&#39;s exactly what I was going to look for. reactiveValues in global.R means that all of the user sessions are sharing the same copies of the variables and triggering each others&#39; reactivity. Sometimes that is actually what you want, but not often!<div><br></div><div>Glad you were able to figure it out!</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Wed, Aug 5, 2015 at 9:47 PM Amber James &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">amber....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">As an update, I pulled the old version of my application and added my updates to that. I made my changes 1 by 1 to check the run time issues. Then, finally, I found the supposed little bugger. The issue seems to have stemmed from my global class. When I was performing code clean up I decided to put my reactiveValues into the global.R file so that the server only contained observe and observeEvent functions. Once I moved this back to the server itself the issues went away. HUZZAH!<div><br></div><div>Sorry about all the confusion. :D Thank you for the assistance.</div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
